00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:42.330
we don't actually need
to migrate a database.

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.490
It's a static app.

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:50.670
But in our YAML, all we're doing
is doing a echo essentially.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:53.880
So what this would do if you're
using something like Rails,

00:10:53.880 --> 00:10:59.030
this would be like a
bundle execute DB migrate.

00:10:59.030 --> 00:11:02.960
Or if you were using any
other sort of migration tool,

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:05.720
you could make sure that
your Kubernetes cluster only

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:07.580
ran that once.

00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:10.520
So some people like
to run the migrations

00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:16.160
on every container that's coming
up for like a web process.

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:19.090
That can lead to issues
where you have contention.

00:11:19.090 --> 00:11:22.880
So you have multiple processes
running your container.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.490
By putting this into
a Kubernetes job,

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:29.480
we make sure that
his only runs once.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.820
And what that actually
looks like in Cloud Build

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:38.180
is we delete the job
itself to make sure

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:42.030
that once it ran once
and was successful,

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:44.900
we have to delete it so we
can run it the second time.

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:47.600
And then in the second
example here, all we're doing

00:11:47.600 --> 00:11:50.510
is running Apply and
creating that new job

00:11:50.510 --> 00:11:52.310
and making sure that it runs.

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:55.490
Now, a lot of our
apps, you might

00:11:55.490 --> 00:12:00.230
have to wait to actually
deploy and update your cluster

00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:03.650
until your migrations are done.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:06.657
In a traditional setup like
this, you'd say, OK, well,

00:12:06.657 --> 00:12:07.740
I'm going to execute that.

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:09.671
How do I actually wait for that?

00:12:09.671 --> 00:12:11.420
And that's where the
beauty of Cloud Build

00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:13.640
comes in, where you might
say, OK, well, now I

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:17.510
can't just have a very
simple straightforward file.

00:12:17.510 --> 00:12:19.640
Luckily for us, you
can extend Cloud Build

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:21.720
to do whatever you want.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:25.160
And we have a bunch of sort
of pre-built Docker containers

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.570
that do actions for you.

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.390
So we're going to
talk about one that I

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:33.560
wrote called kubectl wait for.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:40.230
And I thought I had it
open, but there we go.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:44.400
So kubectl wait for job does
exactly what it sounds like.

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:50.500
It waits for a job to complete
before it lets you continue on.

00:12:50.500 --> 00:12:53.160
So this is literally
a Bash script

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.980
that is wrapped in
a Docker file that

00:12:55.980 --> 00:13:01.290
runs that is passed in the
argument of your job name.

00:13:01.290 --> 00:13:04.140
So this basically
tells Cloud Build, hey,

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:05.890
I'm going to run this job.

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:09.990
But I want to wait to
actually do anything else

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:12.820
until this job is complete.

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:17.550
So just a bunch of Bash foo that
is essentially wrapping kubectl

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:22.800
get jobs and is just pulling
for that to actually be done.

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:25.230
And Cloud Build handles
all of this for you.

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:29.340
Once this is created
in your projects,

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:31.270
you can just pull that down.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:33.075
And we'll show how that works.

00:13:35.830 --> 00:13:40.770
So locally I've checked out
