00:18:34.760 --> 00:18:37.130
So I apparently
live under a rock.

00:18:39.621 --> 00:18:40.120
OK.

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:45.050
Let's try another tab
because that sometimes works.

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:49.650
OK, cool.

00:18:49.650 --> 00:18:53.220
Look, my Wi-Fi
hotspot came in handy.

00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:53.820
Fantastic.

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:58.270
So now we're going to
head back into our app,

00:18:58.270 --> 00:19:03.179
and we're going to
check out the next step

00:19:03.179 --> 00:19:04.770
to remember what
step we're on now.

00:19:13.720 --> 00:19:16.960
And this will update.

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:18.590
There we go, kubectl wait for.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:24.930
So what this is waiting for is
for the prior job to complete.

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:28.940
So you see, in Cloud
Build we have these IDs?

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:33.520
So every step optionally can
have an ID associated with it.

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:41.090
So by default, Cloud Build will
just run your jobs from top

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:44.000
to bottom, unless you
specify that it can do things

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:48.040
parallely, which you
should have it do

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:50.630
so that things happen faster.

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:52.790
And in those cases,
you might still

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:56.150
have cases where you want to
say, hey, I want this step

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:57.420
to happen before this step.

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.360
But I don't really care
where in the whole file

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:02.280
this should actually run.

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:08.840
So you can put together
as-complicated-as-you-want

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:11.990
before this, after
this statements.

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:13.640
And that's what you
use these IDs for.

00:20:13.640 --> 00:20:18.770
So like in this case, if I
wanted to, for the assets,

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:21.650
I could say, wait,
my public assets

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:25.980
only get generated when
Maven package gets run.

00:20:25.980 --> 00:20:29.940
So I want to make sure that I
had the latest ones available.

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:34.610
So I could do wait
for build image.

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:37.670
And then instead of
running those parallely,

00:20:37.670 --> 00:20:41.450
if I had that
enabled, I can instead

00:20:41.450 --> 00:20:45.180
make sure that the latest
assets are actually up to date.

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:47.700
And that's very important
in this example.

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:50.540
When you have something that's
supposed to wait for something,

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:56.220
you actually need to have
that step to actually happen.

00:20:56.220 --> 00:21:00.660
So you might have noticed that--
cool-- so I have this wait for.

00:21:00.660 --> 00:21:02.540
But now I still
haven't done anything.

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:05.330
I still haven't
actually deployed.

00:21:05.330 --> 00:21:08.840
So I built an image.

00:21:08.840 --> 00:21:11.000
I've migrated my database.

00:21:11.000 --> 00:21:12.210
I ran my tests.

00:21:12.210 --> 00:21:14.420
That happens in
package, so that will

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:18.620
fail if my tests don't work.

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:25.490
But we still actually have to go
and update our initial manifest

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:28.410
with our latest image.

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:31.790
So in our next
step, we'll actually

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:38.155
just patch our manifest,
stash our changes.

00:21:44.705 --> 00:21:45.330
And here we go.

00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:50.340
So very simply, in use
cases where you can just

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:54.930
update the image and
let Kubernetes handle

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:58.770
taking the containers, pulling
them out, and actually updating

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:01.390
them, this works really well.

00:22:01.390 --> 00:22:05.580
So we know our database
is in a good state now.

00:22:05.580 --> 00:22:07.500
Now we need all
of our containers

00:22:07.500 --> 00:22:09.240
to have the latest code.

00:22:09.240 --> 00:22:13.710
So we go ahead and we patch
that manifest that's running.

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:15.330
And right now we're
still just going

00:22:15.330 --> 00:22:18.510
to update that image
to just be the latest

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:22.440
version of Pet Clinic that we
generated in our first step.

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:28.010
So this is all well and good.

00:22:28.010 --> 00:22:31.270
But at the end of
the day, you probably

00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:35.950
don't want to have to
run Container Build

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:39.310
Submit every time you
run a CI/CD pipeline.

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:43.090
That kind of defeats
the purpose, right?

00:22:43.090 --> 00:22:45.760
So I'm going to kick
this off, and I'll

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:48.160
show you guys how
to actually run this

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:52.490
every time your code
is actually executed.

00:22:52.490 --> 00:22:55.198
So we had this thing
called Build Triggers--

00:22:58.965 --> 00:22:59.590
Build Triggers.

00:23:04.480 --> 00:23:06.310
So this is where
sort of the history--

00:23:06.310 --> 00:23:08.351
so you can see that I
just kicked that off.

00:23:08.351 --> 00:23:09.850
And you can see
that that's actually

00:23:09.850 --> 00:23:11.920
running in the interface now.

00:23:11.920 --> 00:23:15.760
So here are each of the
steps that we have so far.

00:23:15.760 --> 00:23:18.000
And you actually can
just get the latest log.

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:21.020
So in addition to being
available in the interface,

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:25.610
you can also tail that from
the build details itself.

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:27.640
And if you want to
download the logs,

00:23:27.640 --> 00:23:30.220
load them into
BigQuery or something,

00:23:30.220 --> 00:23:32.740
you can do that
from here as well.

00:23:32.740 --> 00:23:36.880
And you can see the images
that are actually generated.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:41.300
So Build Triggers--
so a Build Trigger

00:23:41.300 --> 00:23:42.930
is exactly what it sounds like.

00:23:42.930 --> 00:23:46.640
It's a trigger for
running your Cloud Build.

00:23:46.640 --> 00:23:48.460
So I have two examples here.

00:23:48.460 --> 00:23:50.840
One is hooked up to GitHub.

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:54.520
So if you push your
code up to GitHub,

00:23:54.520 --> 00:23:59.070
this will automatically
grab that and run it.

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:04.040
So we'll show what a Build
Trigger actually looks like.

00:24:04.040 --> 00:24:08.530
So you can trigger it, branch,
tag, whatever you want.

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:10.130
You can give it some regex.

00:24:10.130 --> 00:24:13.820
You can say, only for
something like a Java app,

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:16.610
maybe you only want Cloud Build
to run when one of your source

00:24:16.610 --> 00:24:21.620
files changes, not when
you edit your README.

00:24:21.620 --> 00:24:24.560
So that can be
pretty helpful if you

00:24:24.560 --> 00:24:27.680
care about not doing excessive
deployments when you're just

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:30.040
doing cosmetic changes.

00:24:30.040 --> 00:24:31.780
And you can
substitute variables.

00:24:31.780 --> 00:24:36.140
So as you saw back here,
we have all these things

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:38.600
that are just dying.

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:42.770
As like a good coder, it's like,
wait, you're specifying things.

00:24:42.770 --> 00:24:44.870
What if I want to
change that later?

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:47.360
That doesn't seem like
the best place for that.

00:24:47.360 --> 00:24:51.890
So you can actually specify
things like your cluster,

00:24:51.890 --> 00:24:58.970
your zone, other variables
from your Build Trigger itself.

00:24:58.970 --> 00:25:01.790
So you could have a
trigger for deploying

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:07.790
to dev, staging, prod, and
put in your substitutes

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:10.080
as required.

00:25:10.080 --> 00:25:12.270
All it is is a matter
of just putting add item

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:15.420
and specifying the variable.

00:25:15.420 --> 00:25:20.020
So we'll go ahead
and let this run.

00:25:20.020 --> 00:25:23.320
And then we'll show
how that works.

00:25:23.320 --> 00:25:25.170
And so you can do
this on GitHub.

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:28.650
You can hook it up to
Bitbucket, in addition

00:25:28.650 --> 00:25:32.890
to source repositories.

00:25:32.890 --> 00:25:35.280
So those are the
three options you

00:25:35.280 --> 00:25:37.272
have to actually trigger these.

00:25:37.272 --> 00:25:38.730
Of course, you
could also, sort of,

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:43.410
build your own trigger by
basically just taking your code

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:51.119
and pushing it to a cloud
source through some other means.

00:25:51.119 --> 00:25:53.160
And if you wanted to just
manually trigger these,

00:25:53.160 --> 00:25:56.790
you can this a
click Run Trigger .

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:59.934
And here we are back
still running this.

00:25:59.934 --> 00:26:01.350
So the other thing
to look at here

00:26:01.350 --> 00:26:04.410
is we are currently
running this,

00:26:04.410 --> 00:26:06.930
and we're always
building to latest.

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:10.410
So that's not really
a best practice.

00:26:10.410 --> 00:26:13.140
This would mean that every
time you ran a build,

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:17.560
you'd be telling your Kubernetes
instances to just run that one.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:19.010
And that works great in dev.

00:26:19.010 --> 00:26:21.960
It doesn't work
that well in prod

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:24.990
when you want to
actually test your code

00:26:24.990 --> 00:26:28.450
prior to having it be deployed.

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:32.185
So in a second, we'll actually
show how that gets updated.

00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:38.090
I'm still in the build.

00:26:38.090 --> 00:26:40.710
I'll just open up the next tab.

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:41.810
There we go.

00:26:41.810 --> 00:26:44.190
This is actually running
through all the tests,

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:46.220
so it takes a second.

00:26:52.700 --> 00:26:54.900
That tab never came
back, unfortunately.

00:27:04.323 --> 00:27:04.960
There we go.

00:27:15.080 --> 00:27:18.320
So in our next step, we'll
actually make that update

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:22.050
so that those substitute
variables can actually be used.

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:25.920
So we'll update in a second.

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:29.670
OK, so we're doing a couple
of things differently here.

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:34.830
One is available to
your cloud trigger

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:40.800
is the shortstraw of Git
Commit that is being built.

00:27:40.800 --> 00:27:43.410
So a lot of times,
you want to be

00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:47.790
able to specify, hey, this
build is attached to this commit

00:27:47.790 --> 00:27:49.600
that I made.

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:51.990
So this is a useful way of
actually checking that out.

00:27:51.990 --> 00:27:56.640
In addition, you probably want
to have some sort of semantic

00:27:56.640 --> 00:28:00.542
to say what that build is
initially being used for.

00:28:00.542 --> 00:28:02.000
So in this case,
we're going to use

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:03.870
something called a deploy tag.

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:07.800
So this would be something
like dev or prod or staging

00:28:07.800 --> 00:28:10.950
so that you'd know, OK,
this was built, tagged

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:13.620
with this new update, and
this is what we're actually

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:15.210
going to deploy that to.

00:28:15.210 --> 00:28:17.510
So if you need to do
a rollback, understand

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:19.160
the history of that build.

00:28:22.210 --> 00:28:24.640
So we have these changes.

00:28:24.640 --> 00:28:27.470
Some of the semantics here
are a little bit different.

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:32.890
So this is if it's just
a dollar sign, that

00:28:32.890 --> 00:28:35.770
means it's provided
directly by Cloud Build.

00:28:35.770 --> 00:28:39.100
If it's a dollar sign
followed by an underscroll,

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:41.350
that means you
substituted it yourself.

00:28:41.350 --> 00:28:45.790
So essentially it's a
poor man's namespacing

00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:49.120
between what Google
gives you when

00:28:49.120 --> 00:28:53.320
you're building it versus
something that you define.

00:28:53.320 --> 00:28:54.980
And you can see that
down here as well.

00:28:54.980 --> 00:28:57.580
So now in our trigger,
we'll have something called

00:28:57.580 --> 00:28:59.620
compute zone and cluster.

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:02.800
That's specified by us as
far as where we actually

00:29:02.800 --> 00:29:04.120
want to deploy the to.

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:08.380
So as long as you have given
Cloud Build the permission

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:12.240
to the project where
that cluster's running,

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:14.610
this will all work.

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:17.430
You can see that
all the way down.

00:29:17.430 --> 00:29:20.610
And then here, instead of
setting the image to latest,

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:25.380
we set it to the deploy tag
that's actually being run.

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:28.200
So let's head back to our
build, see if we've finished.

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:33.570
We failed.

00:29:40.150 --> 00:29:42.800
We will rerun that.

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:44.340
Oh, it's a different one.

00:29:44.340 --> 00:29:48.000
Here, this one succeeded.

00:29:48.000 --> 00:29:51.930
So we'll actually go ahead
and just run our next step.

00:29:51.930 --> 00:29:53.110
That actually includes that.

00:29:53.110 --> 00:29:57.507
So now that we have
these changes initiated,

00:29:57.507 --> 00:29:59.340
we can actually, in
addition to running them

00:29:59.340 --> 00:30:02.220
through Cloud Build, you
can actually test it locally

00:30:02.220 --> 00:30:03.070
as well.

00:30:03.070 --> 00:30:05.726
So I'm going to grab
the substitution there.

00:30:09.764 --> 00:30:11.930
Oh, I many not actually
have the substitution there.

00:30:21.250 --> 00:30:25.030
So it's basically the
same as that submit.

00:30:25.030 --> 00:30:31.010
And then we can actually
specify substitutions.

00:30:31.010 --> 00:30:34.400
Hm, where did I put that?

00:30:34.400 --> 00:30:41.000
So, yeah, so it's this
submit substitutions

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:43.400
equals, and then you
actually can specify

00:30:43.400 --> 00:30:44.430
what you want to change.

00:30:44.430 --> 00:30:47.410
So in our case, we have
a couple of things.

00:30:47.410 --> 00:30:48.830
So we have a deploy tag.

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:53.396
So we can say deploy
tag is equal to dev.

00:30:53.396 --> 00:30:54.815
AUDIENCE: You're missing a d.

00:30:54.815 --> 00:30:56.065
SCOTT FEINBERG: Oh, thank you.

00:30:59.080 --> 00:31:00.280
Spelling's hard.

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:03.242
It's too early in the morning.

00:31:03.242 --> 00:31:04.450
I'm still on East Coast time.

00:31:07.570 --> 00:31:09.100
So we have compute zone.

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:15.104
So we ran our zone
in us-central-c.

00:31:15.104 --> 00:31:17.480
It's going to yell at
me for the underscroll.

00:31:25.530 --> 00:31:27.487
And in the actual
Build Trigger, you

00:31:27.487 --> 00:31:28.820
can actually run these yourself.

00:31:28.820 --> 00:31:31.028
But it's pretty useful to
be able to run this locally

00:31:31.028 --> 00:31:34.220
several times without
having to push it up,

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:38.720
also just being able to run your
build without having to go back

00:31:38.720 --> 00:31:43.374
to the interface, committing
nonsense, things like that.

00:31:43.374 --> 00:31:45.290
So it gives you the
opportunity to really play

00:31:45.290 --> 00:31:48.770
with the build file.

00:31:48.770 --> 00:31:54.975
And also when GitHub goes
down, you can still run it.

00:31:54.975 --> 00:31:55.850
And what else we got?

00:32:00.942 --> 00:32:01.900
What was the other one?

00:32:01.900 --> 00:32:02.850
AUDIENCE: Cluster.

00:32:02.850 --> 00:32:04.376
SCOTT FEINBERG:
Cluster, thank you.

00:32:08.024 --> 00:32:10.400
I should have you
guys write this.

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:15.240
Cluster-- I think it's Scott's
Pet Clinic with an extra dash.

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:17.610
I spell my name with two Ts.

00:32:17.610 --> 00:32:18.920
So I go ahead and run this.

00:32:18.920 --> 00:32:19.920
Let's see if it lets me.

00:32:31.980 --> 00:32:34.530
So this is going to do that
same thing-- tar up the file,

00:32:34.530 --> 00:32:38.140
upload it, and actually run it.

00:32:38.140 --> 00:32:41.180
So Cloud Build, at
the end of the day--

00:32:41.180 --> 00:32:43.990
oh, cool, invalid name.

00:32:43.990 --> 00:32:45.410
Great.

00:32:45.410 --> 00:32:46.360
Oh, yeah.

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:48.480
So this shortstraw is
not available locally.

00:32:48.480 --> 00:32:50.750
So that's why that
error happened.

00:32:50.750 --> 00:32:52.910
So I can actually--

00:32:52.910 --> 00:32:54.590
so while it's not
available locally,

00:32:54.590 --> 00:32:56.650
I can actually
still substitute it.

00:32:56.650 --> 00:33:01.500
So I can still go shortstraw
equals whatever, 1, 2, 3.

00:33:04.680 --> 00:33:06.164
And that'll get around that.

00:33:09.000 --> 00:33:12.580
Or I can actually look at
what my shortstraw is, which

00:33:12.580 --> 00:33:14.760
would be the other option.

00:33:14.760 --> 00:33:16.620
So this uploads it.

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:19.294
Google's actually going
to run it on a fresh VM.

00:33:22.260 --> 00:33:25.010
And there it kicks off.

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:31.090
So that, in a nutshell,
is Cloud Build.

00:33:31.090 --> 00:33:34.450
So it's very simple.

00:33:34.450 --> 00:33:36.920
It's very straightforward.

00:33:36.920 --> 00:33:39.380
It's excellent for these
sorts of use cases, where

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:45.700
you have a small app that you're
not worried about deploying.

00:33:45.700 --> 00:33:49.870
Doing like a blue-green,
you're able to trust Kubernetes

00:33:49.870 --> 00:33:51.760
to deploy it for you.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:55.090
And hopefully you have
some sort of test suite

00:33:55.090 --> 00:33:59.020
that is going to let you
actually accomplish CI/CD.

00:33:59.020 --> 00:34:02.290
Other great benefits of Cloud
Build for doing these sorts

00:34:02.290 --> 00:34:05.230
of things, especially
for small apps,

00:34:05.230 --> 00:34:09.370
is that if you don't have
an existing CI/CD set up,

00:34:09.370 --> 00:34:13.659
or perhaps you don't want to
spin up a new slave for Jenkins

00:34:13.659 --> 00:34:18.100
for a new app, Cloud
Build is pay-as-you-go,

00:34:18.100 --> 00:34:22.389
and not just in the same sense
as traditional resources.

00:34:22.389 --> 00:34:26.090
You pay by the second that
you're build actually runs.

00:34:26.090 --> 00:34:29.360
So I think every day you get
about 100 minutes for free

00:34:29.360 --> 00:34:30.909
of Cloud Build.

00:34:30.909 --> 00:34:34.929
And then you pay some number
of pennies after that.

00:34:34.929 --> 00:34:37.360
But it's fully managed.

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:39.460
You don't have static hardware.

00:34:39.460 --> 00:34:46.239
And most importantly, you're not
troubleshooting your CI/CD test

00:34:46.239 --> 00:34:51.440
runners and slaves when
they happen to go down.

00:34:51.440 --> 00:34:53.510
So you don't have to
actually like maintain this.

00:34:53.510 --> 00:34:59.810
All you're maintaining is
a YAML file in your repo.

00:34:59.810 --> 00:35:03.470
And that's Cloud Build.

00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:06.590
If anyone has any
questions on Cloud Build

00:35:06.590 --> 00:35:08.690
or anything like
that, we have mics

00:35:08.690 --> 00:35:10.700
set up, if anyone has anything.

00:35:23.345 --> 00:35:26.365
AUDIENCE: Does it help to have
an API kick it off remotely?

00:35:26.365 --> 00:35:27.240
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah.

00:35:27.240 --> 00:35:30.480
So what you're
seeing me do here,

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:34.270
this gcloud is just a
wrapper for an API call.

00:35:34.270 --> 00:35:35.220
So it's all available.

00:35:35.220 --> 00:35:37.950
As long as you have a service
account that can hit it,

00:35:37.950 --> 00:35:38.877
you can kick it off.

00:35:38.877 --> 00:35:39.710
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:35:39.710 --> 00:35:40.834
SCOTT FEINBERG: No problem.

00:35:43.380 --> 00:35:45.960
AUDIENCE: Is there anything
built in for notifications,

00:35:45.960 --> 00:35:48.120
like updating status
in GitHub or something

00:35:48.120 --> 00:35:50.994
like that when a build
succeeds or fails?

00:35:50.994 --> 00:35:52.410
SCOTT FEINBERG:
It's not built in.

00:35:52.410 --> 00:35:54.243
But there are a couple
of community projects

00:35:54.243 --> 00:35:56.160
that essentially do that.

00:35:56.160 --> 00:35:59.600
So you can basically add
a tag in the same way.

00:35:59.600 --> 00:36:01.560
Yeah.

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:05.160
AUDIENCE: Can you trigger the
build from a local Bitbucket

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:06.484
repo?

00:36:06.484 --> 00:36:08.340
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah.

00:36:08.340 --> 00:36:11.580
So you can kick it off
from a local Git repo.

00:36:11.580 --> 00:36:13.620
That's actually
what I'm doing here.

00:36:13.620 --> 00:36:18.720
You can't run Cloud Builder
locally in that sense,

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:20.880
in the sense of like
Cloud Build itself is not

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:24.150
executing locally.

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:26.340
It's just easier to
just load it out.

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:30.810
AUDIENCE: I mean pushing to a
Bitbucket repo from Atlassian,

00:36:30.810 --> 00:36:34.165
and then the trigger triggers
the build on Cloud Builder.

00:36:34.165 --> 00:36:35.040
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah.

00:36:35.040 --> 00:36:36.870
So that's built in.

00:36:36.870 --> 00:36:39.060
So you actually just
have to just link it.

00:36:39.060 --> 00:36:42.540
And then every time you
commit to Bitbucket,

00:36:42.540 --> 00:36:46.020
it'll kick off the build.

00:36:46.020 --> 00:36:48.050
AUDIENCE: Hey,
Scott, great talk.

00:36:48.050 --> 00:36:49.670
Can you talk about
secret management?

00:36:49.670 --> 00:36:52.439
And if I'm injecting
secrets into the flow,

00:36:52.439 --> 00:36:54.230
would it actually come
up through the logs,

00:36:54.230 --> 00:36:55.400
or there's any like--

00:36:55.400 --> 00:36:56.275
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah.

00:36:56.275 --> 00:37:00.740
So if you have actual secrets
that you want to deploy,

00:37:00.740 --> 00:37:04.800
those should be living within
your Kubernetes secrets file.

00:37:04.800 --> 00:37:07.670
So I can actually show
you how that works.

00:37:07.670 --> 00:37:11.600
I have secrets in here that
are protecting the Cloud SQL

00:37:11.600 --> 00:37:12.890
credentials.

00:37:12.890 --> 00:37:16.184
So if you look at the
configuration here--

00:37:16.184 --> 00:37:17.100
so it loads for a sec.

00:37:17.100 --> 00:37:19.950
So I have these secrets.

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:21.890
They don't show you--

00:37:21.890 --> 00:37:24.300
like I couldn't make
this show if I wanted to.

00:37:24.300 --> 00:37:28.140
I would have to
download it on Base64

00:37:28.140 --> 00:37:32.080
and code it to actually
view what these things are.

00:37:32.080 --> 00:37:37.170
But substitutions itself do
eventually show up in the logs.

00:37:37.170 --> 00:37:39.540
So to actually use
secrets you actually

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:43.690
want to have that
run on Kubernetes

00:37:43.690 --> 00:37:45.820
and execute it there.

00:37:45.820 --> 00:37:49.290
That's one of the reasons that,
like, running your migrations,

00:37:49.290 --> 00:37:51.540
you should actually do that
on the Kubernetes cluster

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:53.040
not on Cloud Build
because you don't

00:37:53.040 --> 00:37:55.650
want to give Cloud
Build your secrets.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:38:00.019
And it gives you one place
to actually manage those.

00:38:00.019 --> 00:38:00.560
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:38:00.560 --> 00:38:03.110
Migration from other
build systems-- are there

00:38:03.110 --> 00:38:05.000
any tools there,
or are you planning

00:38:05.000 --> 00:38:07.486
to make any tools
that does that?

00:38:07.486 --> 00:38:09.360
SCOTT FEINBERG: To
migrate from which system?

00:38:09.360 --> 00:38:11.360
AUDIENCE: For other static
system, for instance,

00:38:11.360 --> 00:38:12.770
Jenkins, Circle, whatever.

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:14.186
SCOTT FEINBERG:
There's not a tool

00:38:14.186 --> 00:38:17.540
that would essentially
like do it for you,

00:38:17.540 --> 00:38:20.240
like rewrite your pipeline.

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:25.160
But it's something that
if in Jenkins you're

00:38:25.160 --> 00:38:26.900
just running a bunch
of Bash scripts,

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:31.945
you can execute each of those
Bash steps within Cloud Build.

00:38:31.945 --> 00:38:33.320
AUDIENCE: Yes,
that I understand.

00:38:33.320 --> 00:38:34.736
SCOTT FEINBERG:
You could actually

00:38:34.736 --> 00:38:37.670
take the whole Bash script,
throw it in a Docker container,

00:38:37.670 --> 00:38:40.484
and execute it on Cloud
Build if you wanted to.

00:38:40.484 --> 00:38:41.900
AUDIENCE: Yeah, I
understand that.

00:38:41.900 --> 00:38:44.510
I'm thinking more of the
scenarios like I'm a company,

00:38:44.510 --> 00:38:48.860
and I have 200 repos.

00:38:48.860 --> 00:38:51.800
It's going to be quite an effort
to migrate each in one of them.

00:38:51.800 --> 00:38:52.675
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah.

00:38:52.675 --> 00:38:56.000
AUDIENCE: But another
question, is there a way--

00:38:56.000 --> 00:38:57.530
if I understand
correctly, you need

00:38:57.530 --> 00:38:59.651
to preconfigure the pipeline.

00:38:59.651 --> 00:39:00.650
Are you-- just a moment.

00:39:00.650 --> 00:39:01.524
AUDIENCE: No problem.

00:39:01.524 --> 00:39:03.590
AUDIENCE: You need to
preconfigure the pipeline

00:39:03.590 --> 00:39:06.020
to actually does that.

00:39:06.020 --> 00:39:09.950
Now, let's say the user wants
to change the pipeline as part

00:39:09.950 --> 00:39:11.190
of the PR itself.

00:39:11.190 --> 00:39:12.660
Is it possible?

00:39:12.660 --> 00:39:14.410
SCOTT FEINBERG: Change
what on the server?

00:39:14.410 --> 00:39:15.368
AUDIENCE: The pipeline.

00:39:15.368 --> 00:39:18.012
Let's say you have a Jenkins
file or something like that,

00:39:18.012 --> 00:39:19.370
God forbid.

00:39:19.370 --> 00:39:24.400
And you actually go, and
it's part of changing.

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:26.150
You want to change the
CI pipeline itself,

00:39:26.150 --> 00:39:28.910
and you want to test it
as part of the PR itself,

00:39:28.910 --> 00:39:29.960
let's say for GitHub.

00:39:29.960 --> 00:39:32.767
Is it possible to do the
same thing with Cloud Build?

00:39:32.767 --> 00:39:34.350
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah,
so Cloud Build--

00:39:34.350 --> 00:39:37.320
so- the YAML file is just
a part of your Git repo.

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:41.600
So if you wanted to experiment
with it prior to deploying,

00:39:41.600 --> 00:39:43.130
you could do that locally.

00:39:43.130 --> 00:39:44.630
But then if you
wanted to actually--

00:39:44.630 --> 00:39:48.980
you could just commit, have the
cloud trigger do it as well.

00:39:48.980 --> 00:39:49.980
AUDIENCE: OK, thank you.

00:39:49.980 --> 00:39:50.925
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah.

00:39:50.925 --> 00:39:51.800
AUDIENCE: Hey, Scott.

00:39:51.800 --> 00:39:52.400
Great talk.

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:53.030
Great demo.

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:55.370
I had a question,
just real quick ones.

00:39:55.370 --> 00:39:57.830
Is that example
repository going to be

00:39:57.830 --> 00:40:01.897
online or publicly exposed, and
also, the kubectl wait for job?

00:40:01.897 --> 00:40:03.105
Just as a backward reference.

00:40:03.105 --> 00:40:06.060
SCOTT FEINBERG: So kubectl
wait for job is public.

00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:09.930
So if you just go to Cloud
Builders dash community,

00:40:09.930 --> 00:40:10.880
it is right in there.

00:40:10.880 --> 00:40:13.280
So this is sort of the--

00:40:13.280 --> 00:40:14.780
think of it like
the Blast community

00:40:14.780 --> 00:40:17.420
repo with a bunch of stuff.

00:40:17.420 --> 00:40:19.370
So you have Helm
in here, Firebase,

00:40:19.370 --> 00:40:21.110
a bunch of really good stuff.

00:40:21.110 --> 00:40:24.700
And my sample code
will be available.

00:40:24.700 --> 00:40:27.120
I'm not positive
which GitHub account.

00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:28.287
Currently, it's on mine, so.

00:40:28.287 --> 00:40:29.286
AUDIENCE: OK, thank you.

00:40:29.286 --> 00:40:30.565
SCOTT FEINBERG: No problem.

00:40:30.565 --> 00:40:31.106
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:40:31.106 --> 00:40:32.890
Thanks for the demo.

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:34.350
So I have a few questions.

00:40:34.350 --> 00:40:36.620
The first one, in
the UI, you were

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:39.410
triggering some-- you were
configuring the triggers.

00:40:39.410 --> 00:40:41.370
Is it also available
in the YAML file

00:40:41.370 --> 00:40:44.520
so you can do that all
declaratively in a file?

00:40:44.520 --> 00:40:46.790
SCOTT FEINBERG: There's not yet.

00:40:46.790 --> 00:40:49.750
I believe someone is
hopefully working on that.

00:40:49.750 --> 00:40:52.369
But, yeah, that would be
a really nice feature.

00:40:52.369 --> 00:40:54.410
AUDIENCE: And then for
the actual jobs, are there

00:40:54.410 --> 00:40:56.630
any guarantees on performance?

00:40:56.630 --> 00:40:59.090
Can we expect a similar
performance every time

00:40:59.090 --> 00:41:01.245
we run the jobs for
our measurements?

00:41:01.245 --> 00:41:02.870
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah,
so you can expect

00:41:02.870 --> 00:41:05.060
that the job will execute
the same because it's

00:41:05.060 --> 00:41:07.190
a fresh VM of the same size.

00:41:07.190 --> 00:41:09.650
You can also specify
different size VMs.

00:41:09.650 --> 00:41:14.150
So let's say that you have
something really, really beefy

00:41:14.150 --> 00:41:17.660
and you want to run a bunch
of parallel threads in it,

00:41:17.660 --> 00:41:18.620
you can do that.

00:41:18.620 --> 00:41:21.150
And you can use as big as
an instance as you want.

00:41:21.150 --> 00:41:23.552
You can just specify
that in the Cloud Build.

00:41:23.552 --> 00:41:24.260
AUDIENCE: Thanks.

00:41:24.260 --> 00:41:26.650
And sorry, one last
question, is there a way--

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:29.210
if you have a lot of jobs
coming in from a lot of commits

00:41:29.210 --> 00:41:31.580
at the same time, is there
a way to queue up and say

00:41:31.580 --> 00:41:34.820
there's only one kind of
deploy job happening at once,

00:41:34.820 --> 00:41:37.190
or maybe there's only one
kind of integration happening

00:41:37.190 --> 00:41:40.190
to, like, block the
deployment from happening

00:41:40.190 --> 00:41:42.099
during an integration test?

00:41:42.099 --> 00:41:43.640
SCOTT FEINBERG:
Yeah, so there's no--

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:46.640
so you could run an integration
test in the same Cloud Build

00:41:46.640 --> 00:41:48.380
prior to actually deploying.

00:41:48.380 --> 00:41:52.400
So that's what that sort of
wait for example, works well.

00:41:52.400 --> 00:41:58.370
As far as like a cross
Cloud Build orchestration,

00:41:58.370 --> 00:42:00.319
that doesn't exist,
as far as I know.

00:42:00.319 --> 00:42:00.860
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:42:00.860 --> 00:42:01.360
Thank you.

00:42:01.360 --> 00:42:03.664
SCOTT FEINBERG: No problem.

00:42:03.664 --> 00:42:04.455
AUDIENCE: Hi there.

00:42:04.455 --> 00:42:07.079
Did a quick search, and it looks
like it's only for Kubernetes.

00:42:07.079 --> 00:42:10.037
Is there plans to bring this to
like Functions or App Engine?

00:42:10.037 --> 00:42:12.370
SCOTT FEINBERG: So you can
use Cloud Build for anything.

00:42:12.370 --> 00:42:12.970
AUDIENCE: Oh, you can?

00:42:12.970 --> 00:42:13.844
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah.

00:42:13.844 --> 00:42:16.150
So it's like-- just think
of it like Docker containers

00:42:16.150 --> 00:42:18.580
in the cloud.

00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:20.980
So you could run
gcloud app deploy

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:26.150
for App Engine, whatever the
command is for Functions.

00:42:26.150 --> 00:42:27.460
So you can use it for anything.

00:42:27.460 --> 00:42:29.910
It's really for
those cases where--

00:42:29.910 --> 00:42:32.230
like with Functions,
where you're like,

00:42:32.230 --> 00:42:33.959
great, so I wrote
this little function.

00:42:33.959 --> 00:42:35.500
I really don't want
to have to set up

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:37.560
a whole, like,
Jenkins setup just

00:42:37.560 --> 00:42:40.900
to deploy these
six lines of code.

00:42:40.900 --> 00:42:42.940
I can just put a
Cloud Build file.

00:42:42.940 --> 00:42:45.380
And it'll take two seconds,
and it'll just be like--

00:42:45.380 --> 00:42:47.230
essentially just
like the deploy step.

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:47.940
And that's it.

00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:48.310
AUDIENCE: Sweet.

00:42:48.310 --> 00:42:48.640
Thank you.

00:42:48.640 --> 00:42:49.515
SCOTT FEINBERG: Yeah.

00:42:49.515 --> 00:42:50.540
No problem.

00:42:50.540 --> 00:42:53.200
Any other questions?

00:42:53.200 --> 00:42:53.700
Awesome.

00:42:53.700 --> 00:42:54.490
Thanks, everybody.

00:42:54.490 --> 00:42:57.540
